## Project structure

```
src/
  app/    -- suggested layout: organize src/app however you like
    components/
{{#if extension}}
      app/        -- the root component
{{/if}}
    services/
{{#if extension}}
      chrome-api/ -- the one place that touches chrome.*
{{else}}
    views/
    routes.ts
{{/if}}
    main.{{lang}}
  assets/
  core/   -- Nutin framework source
{{#if extension}}
  extension/ -- manifest.json and background.{{lang}}
{{/if}}
  styles/

tools/    -- build, {{#unless extension}}development, {{/unless}}generator and testing

nutin.config.js   -- options and configuration
```

{{#if extension}}
This project is a browser extension: its page is the {{#if (eq extension "popup")}}toolbar popup{{else}}side panel (Firefox: the sidebar){{/if}}, with no router. `main.{{lang}}` mounts the root component, `AppComponent`, which composes the page from its children.

* Components **encapsulate UI behavior**
* Services **handle application concerns** through shared instances
{{else}}
* Views **orchestrate application flow**
* Components **encapsulate UI behavior**
* Services **handle application concerns** through shared instances
{{/if}}

### Imports

- Imports use **relative paths** and end with `.js` extension.

```js
import { Foo } from 'relative/path/to/foo.js';
```

- Use Nutin's code barrel file (`src/core/index.js`) to import Nutin's modules.
{{#if (eq lang "ts")}}
- Nutin's types (`Nutin.ComponentConfig`, {{#unless extension}}`Nutin.Routes`, {{/unless}}`Nutin.Template`, …) live in the global `Nutin` namespace declared in `src/core/internals.d.ts`: no import needed.
{{/if}}

## How do I run my app?

```bash
{{#unless extension}}
# Development
{{packageManager}} run serve # build and serve
{{packageManager}} run dev   # build and serve with live reload
{{packageManager}} run dev -- --port 3000 # serve on another port (default 9090; PORT=3000 also works)

{{/unless}}
# Build
{{packageManager}} run build      # build the application
{{packageManager}} run build:prod # build the application for production
{{#if (eq lang "ts")}}
{{packageManager}} run typecheck  # type-check only, without building
{{/if}}
{{#if extension}}

# Package for the stores
{{packageManager}} run package:extension  # prod build + dist/<name>-<browser>-<version>.zip
{{packageManager}} run validate:extension # check the packages
{{packageManager}} run lint:extension     # Mozilla's linter, on dist/firefox
{{/if}}
```
{{#if extension}}

The build writes one unpacked extension per browser. Load `dist/chrome` with **Load unpacked** in `chrome://extensions` (Developer mode on), or `dist/firefox/manifest.json` with **Load Temporary Add-on…** in Firefox's `about:debugging`. After each build, reload the extension there, then reopen the {{#if (eq extension "popup")}}popup{{else}}side panel{{/if}}.
{{/if}}
{{#if extension}}

## Browser extension

- **The manifest:** `src/extension/manifest.json` is Chrome's; the build derives Firefox's from it, and sets `version` from `package.json`. Edit the name, description, icons and permissions there. It only asks for `storage`{{#if (eq extension "side-panel")}} and `sidePanel`{{/if}}: add what your extension uses, and nothing more, since each permission needs a justification in the stores. Reading the active tab's URL needs `"tabs"`.
- **The extension APIs:** use `chrome.*` only through `src/app/services/chrome-api/chrome-api.service.{{lang}}`, and add a method there for each new API:

  ```ts
  const { theme } = await chromeApiService.storageGet('sync', ['theme']);
  const off = chromeApiService.onStorageChanged((changes) => { /* ... */ });
  ```

  Its calls can fail (the extension was reloaded under an open {{#if (eq extension "popup")}}popup{{else}}panel{{/if}}, storage is over its quota): show the error in the page rather than leaving it blank.
- **The background script:** `src/extension/background.{{lang}}` is bundled on its own. Chrome runs it as a service worker, Firefox as a background script: no DOM, and no state kept between events.
- **Files the manifest references** (icons, `_locales/`) go in `public/` or `src/assets/`. Bundle everything: no remote scripts or fonts.
- **Debugging:** right-click inside the {{#if (eq extension "popup")}}popup{{else}}side panel{{/if}} and pick **Inspect**. For the background script, use the "service worker" link on the extension's card in `chrome://extensions`, or **Inspect** in Firefox's `about:debugging`.

What ships, packaging, store submissions, `web-ext lint` warnings and limits: [How do I create a browser extension?](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/cli/create-a-browser-extension)

## How do I generate components and services?
{{else}}

## How do I generate components, views and services?
{{/if}}

Elements are generated in `src/app/<path>/`, so you choose the project structure. The generator stops if the target folder already exists and isn't empty.

```bash
{{#if extension}}
{{packageManager}} run generate <component|service> PATH/TO/ELEMENT_NAME
{{else}}
{{packageManager}} run generate <component|view|service> PATH/TO/ELEMENT_NAME
{{/if}}

# Examples
{{packageManager}} run generate component components/hello-world # generates src/app/components/hello-world/hello-world.component.{{lang}}|html|scss
{{#if extension}}
{{packageManager}} run generate service services/settings # generates src/app/services/settings/settings.service.{{lang}}
{{else}}
{{packageManager}} run generate view user/dashboard # generates src/app/user/dashboard/dashboard.view.{{lang}}|html|scss
{{/if}}
{{packageManager}} run generate component components/hello-world --dry-run # lists the files it would generate, without writing them
```

## What does a typical component look like?

```bash
components/
  product-card/
    product-card.component.html
    product-card.component.scss
    product-card.component.ts
```

```ts
import { nutinFor, Navigation, Component } from '../../../core/index.js';

// The component's .html template is injected here.
const templateFn = (product: Product) => html`__TEMPLATE_PLACEHOLDER__`;

export class ProductCardComponent extends Component {
  private _product: Product;

  constructor(mountTarget: HTMLElement, config: Product) {
    super({ templateFn, mountTarget, config });
    this._product = config;
  }

  // Declares children mounted into the data-component / data-catalog placeholders below.
  public override registerChildren(): Nutin.ComponentConfig[] {
    // Single child, matched to <div data-component="product-details">
    const detailsComponent = {
      selector: `product-details`,
      factory: (el) => new ProductDetailsComponent(
        el, 
        { config: this._product.details },
      )
    };

    // One child per item in _product.tags, matched to <div data-catalog="product-tags">
    const tagsCatalog = this.createCatalogComponents({
      selector: 'product-tags',
      items: this._product.tags,
      component: ProductTagsComponent,
      elementName: 'product-tag'
    });

    return [
      detailsComponent,
      ...tagsCatalog
    ];
  }

  // Referenced by the data-event attribute in the template. protected, not private: the editor would flag it as unused.
{{#if extension}}
  // chrome.* goes through chromeApiService (src/app/services/chrome-api/).
  protected _openProductPage(url: string): void {
    void chromeApiService.openTab(url);
  }
{{else}}
  protected _goToProductPage(productId: string): void {
    Navigation.navigateTo(`/product/${productId}`);
  }
{{/if}}
}
```

```html
<div
{{#if extension}}
  data-product-url="${product.url}"
  data-event="click:_openProductPage:@dataset:productUrl"
{{else}}
  data-product-id="${product.id}"
  data-event="click:_goToProductPage:@dataset:productId"
{{/if}}
  class="product-card"
>
  <h2 class="product-card__name">${product.name}</h2>
  <!-- if/else in template: nutinIf(condition, branch).nutinElse(branch) -->
  ${nutinIf(product.available, () => html`<div data-component="product-details"></div>`)
    .nutinElse('Out of stock')
  }
  <!-- use nutinFor() when an item doesn't have its own behaviour or state -->
  ${nutinFor(product.tags, (tag) => html`<li>${tag}</li>`)
    .nutinElse('No tags')
  }
  <div data-catalog="product-tags"></div>
</div>
```

```scss
.product-card {
  // component styles
}

.product-card__name {
  // element styles
}
```

`nutinIf` provides conditional rendering and `nutinFor` iterable rendering.
Both support an optional `.nutinElse()` for fallback content. See [How do I use conditional and iterative rendering?](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/api/use-conditional-and-iterative-rendering).
Native JavaScript expressions and array methods remain fully supported.

A template can also be written directly inline (skip the `.html` file and the placeholder).
{{#if (eq lang "ts")}}The editor then sees what the template's `${}` uses (imports, parameters, helpers).
{{/if}}
Defining both an inline template and an external `.html` file, or neither, fails the build.

## Options and configuration

### Main file

`nutin.config.js` - Main Nutin configuration and tool options. See the inline comments and [How do I configure Nutin?](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/options-and-features/configure-nutin) for details.

This is where you can:

{{#if extension}}
- Set the `extension` block: `browsers` lists the browsers to build for; `geckoId` is Firefox's add-on id. `storage.sync` data and addons.mozilla.org are tied to it, so pick yours before the first release (e.g. `name@your-domain`): it can never change afterwards.
- Turn on [Tailwind CSS](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/options-and-features/use-tailwind-css) by setting `tailwind: true`. The CSS ships in the extension, and Tailwind v4's browser baseline (Chrome 111, Firefox 128) is below the manifest's minimums.
- Configure the builder and the built-in testing toolkit.

The build refuses `i18n` and `generateSEOFiles`: i18n puts the language in the URL (`/<lang>/...`), and SEO files prerender routes, neither of which an extension page has.
{{else}}
- Toggle options ([Tailwind CSS](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/options-and-features/use-tailwind-css), [SEO file generation](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/options-and-features/use-seo-file-generation)).
- List your languages to enable [i18n](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/options-and-features/use-i18n): `i18n: ['en', 'fr']`, the default first (`[]` disables it).
- Configure the builder and the built-in testing toolkit.
{{/if}}
{{#unless extension}}

### Specialized files

- `config/seo.json` - with `generateSEOFiles` enabled. Register your routes' description, title and ogImage here.
{{/unless}}

## Styling

- Nutin uses SASS (`.scss` syntax, compatible with plain CSS) as its default styling solution.

- Tailwind CSS v4 is also supported as an optional utility-first CSS layer. See [How do I use Tailwind CSS?](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/options-and-features/use-tailwind-css)

**Note:** Installing Tailwind CSS dependencies may print `npm warn install-scripts`. This is expected with npm 12's default install-script allowlisting and is safe to ignore.

## Testing

You can use any testing tool you prefer for application code.

This project includes [testin-nutin](https://www.nutin.dev/docs/testing), Nutin's small testing toolkit. Nutin itself is tested exclusively with it, and the tests are included in the generated project so you can:
- use them as executable documentation;
- run and modify Nutin's tests when modifying its code.

Run the tests with:

`{{packageManager}} run testin-nutin`

To use testin-nutin for application tests, enable:

```js
// nutin.config.js
testinNutin: {
  includeApp: true,
}
```
*Note:* TestinNutin files are plain JavaScript and use root imports{{#if (eq lang "ts")}}: they test the compiled output, so they aren't type-checked{{/if}}.
```js
import { MyModule } from '#root/dist/src/path/to/my-module.js';
```
{{#if extension}}

There's no `chrome` in tests: `tools/testin-nutin/mocks/fake-chrome.js` is a stand-in for the APIs `chromeApiService` wraps. Call `installFakeChrome({ url })` before each test, and `uninstallFakeChrome()` after.
{{/if}}

